WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.987
So, we
are the Public Health Group.

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:11.789
And I'm Milind I'm gonna be
presenting on behalf of our

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.397
group.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:16.549
And so, we have some problems
that we thought are ready for

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.599
immediate collaboration.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.081
I'm gonna ask my teammates
to jump in if I misrepresent

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:22.845
anything.

00:00:22.846 --> 00:00:24.887
And so
immediate collaboration and

00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:27.137
then interesting
long term problems.

00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:31.255
So, immediate collaboration is
assignment and scheduling of

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:35.150
health-worker visits with
dynamically generated data.

00:00:35.151 --> 00:00:40.879
So as I understand it,
we have health workers and

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.967
they have to plan routes.

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:48.921
So, each person is assigned
maybe a thousand people have to

00:00:48.922 --> 00:00:51.307
visit on a large geography.

00:00:51.308 --> 00:00:54.014
They have to ideally visit
each and every home but

00:00:54.015 --> 00:00:57.000
that may not work, so
you may do a risk based routing.

00:00:57.001 --> 00:00:58.771
So people who have
a higher risk,

00:00:58.772 --> 00:01:01.248
you want to make sure
that they do get visited.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:06.143
And so this problem becomes one
of a traveling salesman problem,

00:01:06.144 --> 00:01:11.038
but interesting because there's
some places have a higher value

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:12.206
to be visited.

00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:14.902
And then the problem
can be made more and

00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:19.153
more complicated because we may
want to optimize which health

00:01:19.154 --> 00:01:21.208
worker takes on which route.

00:01:21.209 --> 00:01:23.705
The roots may not be
known in advance so

00:01:23.706 --> 00:01:27.216
they may be learning of
the routes as we go on, we might

00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:30.870
want to build on existing
relationships and so forth.

00:01:30.871 --> 00:01:35.526
So we created a four part
evaluation of each problem that

00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:38.498
we discussed, social relevance,

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:42.361
how interesting is it
from an AI perspective,

00:01:42.362 --> 00:01:47.235
collaboration opportunity,
and availability of data.

00:01:47.236 --> 00:01:51.685
And this problem seemed to score
high in terms of all of these

00:01:51.686 --> 00:01:56.046
dimensions, and so this we
consider as our top priority.

00:01:56.047 --> 00:02:00.495
A second problem was risk based
targeting of patients for

00:02:00.496 --> 00:02:03.005
tele advising and monitoring.

00:02:03.006 --> 00:02:06.046
You can see that there is some
overlap here in the risk based

00:02:06.047 --> 00:02:06.675
approach.

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:08.588
So basic idea is that,

00:02:08.589 --> 00:02:12.725
this is a problem where
we have to call patients.

00:02:12.726 --> 00:02:17.111
Unfortunately if you
call all the patients all

00:02:17.112 --> 00:02:21.672
the time it's very
costly to the patients.

00:02:21.673 --> 00:02:27.850
And so can we target which
patients are more risk prone and

00:02:27.851 --> 00:02:30.990
therefore those are the ones you
would target a little bit more.

00:02:30.991 --> 00:02:33.210
And so this is apparently
something where

00:02:33.211 --> 00:02:36.600
the GatesFoundation has data,
and something where

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.660
we may have some contacts
in order to get that data.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.510
The first problem
that I mentioned,

00:02:43.511 --> 00:02:46.390
this assignment problem it seems
like there's already years of

00:02:46.391 --> 00:02:49.060
data available, and so
that's why this seemed to

00:02:49.061 --> 00:02:51.670
be the more interesting problem
to focus on right away.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:55.950
So these two are our problems
for immediate collaboration and

00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:59.650
then longer term possibly
more challenging problems was

00:02:59.651 --> 00:03:01.270
slum health monitoring.

00:03:01.271 --> 00:03:04.550
Here we are trying to geolocate
diseases within a slum,

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:08.850
predict some areas are prone to
certain diseases, and therefore,

00:03:08.851 --> 00:03:12.360
recommend actions to start
medicine, awareness camps,

00:03:12.361 --> 00:03:15.440
screen for
certain diseases in those areas.

00:03:15.441 --> 00:03:18.920
This is also a problem of
portfolio optimization because

00:03:18.921 --> 00:03:24.340
we are trying to figure out
which medicines to stock up on.

00:03:24.341 --> 00:03:25.170
The one and

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:29.530
negative here it seems negative
or is that, it seems like this

00:03:29.531 --> 00:03:31.928
problem has already received
a lot of attention.

00:03:31.929 --> 00:03:36.260
And so as such it
wouldn't be up to us to,

00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:39.600
we wouldn't be necessarily
the first ones to go into this,

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:42.200
we may have to coordinate
with lots of other groups.

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:46.030
But certainly a very important
problem and something that would

00:03:46.031 --> 00:03:51.860
yield a longer term
interesting problem to work on.

00:03:51.861 --> 00:03:54.926
Another one is educating
adolescents on sexual and

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:59.140
reproductive health, and this is
possibly a peer-based approach.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.080
So we might take an approach
similar to the work

00:04:05.081 --> 00:04:09.760
we've done using social
networks to educate people.

00:04:09.761 --> 00:04:14.650
But the cultural context here
may be far more complex.

00:04:14.651 --> 00:04:17.580
And so it wasn't clear if
from an AI perspective,

00:04:17.581 --> 00:04:19.870
it would be as interesting.

00:04:19.871 --> 00:04:22.974
But certainly a very
complicated problem.

00:04:22.975 --> 00:04:26.605
Decision aids for health workers
based on decision trees.

00:04:26.606 --> 00:04:30.898
So we want to have
this health workers ask

00:04:30.899 --> 00:04:34.571
questions to diagnose diseases.

00:04:34.572 --> 00:04:38.110
However, we can't ask
lots of questions, so

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:42.080
what's the right set
of questions to ask?

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.070
How to develop?

00:04:43.071 --> 00:04:45.790
So, there's a POMDP
style framework that

00:04:45.791 --> 00:04:48.390
could be developed to
generate these questions.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.070
It seemed that this
could be a problem,

00:04:54.071 --> 00:04:55.320
that's really important.

00:04:55.321 --> 00:04:59.640
But maybe doesn't need a very
large scale collaboration.

00:04:59.641 --> 00:05:04.390
But we are sort of keeping it in
terms of a problem that will be

00:05:04.391 --> 00:05:05.600
in the future.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:06.624
So go on, please.

00:05:06.625 --> 00:05:10.136
&gt;&gt; I know that Brian Delancy
who used to be at [INAUDIBLE],

00:05:10.137 --> 00:05:11.939
and now he's at Cape Town.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.119
He has been working on such
a system for some time.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:15.918
&gt;&gt; I see okay.

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:18.088
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:05:18.089 --> 00:05:20.863
&gt;&gt; For decision trees and

00:05:20.864 --> 00:05:25.687
using that to [INAUDIBLE]
help workers.

00:05:25.688 --> 00:05:28.630
&gt;&gt; Okay wonderful.

00:05:28.631 --> 00:05:29.380
Okay so

00:05:29.381 --> 00:05:32.890
that's my presentation on behalf
of the Public Health Group.

00:05:32.891 --> 00:05:35.879
Does anybody else from the Group
want to add any comments or

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:36.489
anything?

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:37.480
Okay.

